[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Generics (Type Parameters) in Go"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Go 1.8 introduced generics, a powerful feature that allows you to write code with types that can be specified later. This tutorial covers the fundamentals of type parameters, constraints, and practical applications of generics in Go."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Generics?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before generics, if you wanted to write a function that could sum numbers of different types (e.g., int and float64), you had to write duplicate code for each type or rely on empty interfaces (interface{}), which sacrificed compile-time type safety."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing Your First Generic Function"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To add type parameters to a function, you include a type parameter list in square brackets before the regular function arguments."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

// PrintSlice takes a slice of any comparable type T and prints its elements.
func PrintSlice[T any](s []T) {
    for _, v := range s {
        fmt.Println(v)
    }
}

func main() {
    nums := []int{1, 2, 3}
    strs := []string{"apple", "banana"}

    PrintSlice(nums)
    PrintSlice(strs)
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Type Constraints and the constraints Package"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Often, you do not want your generic functions to accept absolutely any type. You want to restrict them to types that support specific operations, like addition or comparison."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

// Number is a custom interface constraint permitting integers and floats.
type Number interface {
    ~int | ~int64 | ~float64
}

// Sum returns the sum of any slice of Number types.
func Sum[T Number](numbers []T) T {
    var sum T
    for _, n := range numbers {
        sum += n
    }
    return sum
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Generic Data Structures"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics are not just for functions. You can also define generic struct types, which is extremely useful for building data structures like linked lists, stacks, or queues."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"package main

// Node represents a single element in a linked list.
type Node[T any] struct {
    val  T
    next *Node[T]
}

// Push adds a new value to the front of the list.
func (n *Node[T]) Push(val T) *Node[T] {
    return &Node[T]{
        val:  val,
        next: n,
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Go Generics"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not overuse generics. If your code operates on a concrete type and does not duplicate logic, stick to standard types."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use type inference when calling generic functions so Go can automatically determine the type parameter."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage the golang.org/x/exp/constraints package for standard type constraints before writing custom ones."}]
